    "q-221714": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221714",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-1]?$\n\nI. $1$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $-1$",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence class $[-1]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-1$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-1] & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼-1\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{4}=(-1)^{4}\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{4}-1=0\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,({x}^{2}+1)({x}^{2}-1)=0\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{2}-1=0\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=1\\} \\\\  & =\\{-1,1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-1$ and $1$ are in $[-1],$ while $4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
