    "q-76892": {
      "dom_id": "step-q76892",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-\\pi ]?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nIII. $\\pi $",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence class $[-\\pi ]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-\\pi $ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-\\pi ] & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼-\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-2\\pi ,-\\pi ,0,\\pi ,2\\pi ,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $0$ and $\\pi $ are in $[-\\pi ],$ while $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
