    "q-221771": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221771",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\nI. $[0]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{1}{2}]$\n\nIII. $[\\frac{3}{2}]$",
      "worked_solution": "We have $-\\frac{1}{2}\\in [x]$ if $-\\frac{1}{2}∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x \\\\ -1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin [0]$ because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin [\\frac{1}{2}]$ because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}\\in [\\frac{3}{2}]$ because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
