      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $5\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-4?$\n\nI. $[-14]$\n\nII. $[11]$\n\nIII. $[14]$",
      "worked_solution": "We have $-4\\in [x]$ if $-4∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,|\\,(-4-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-4\\in [-14]$ because $5\\,|\\,(-4+14)\\,✓$\n\n- $-4\\in [11]$ because $5\\,|\\,(-4-11)\\,✓$\n\n- $-4\\notin [14]$ because $5∤(-4-14)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
