    "q-221729": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221729",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A=\\{0,1,2,…,9\\},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $7∣(b-a).$   How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼0\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-0)\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣a\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,7\\} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼1\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-1)\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,8\\} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼2\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,9\\} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼3\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-3)\\} \\\\  & =\\{3\\} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼4\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-4)\\} \\\\  & =\\{4\\} \\\\  \\\\ [5] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼5\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-5)\\} \\\\  & =\\{5\\} \\\\  \\\\ [6] & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼6\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A\\,:\\,7∣(a-6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{6\\} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $7$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]=\\{0,7\\},\\,[1]=\\{1,8\\},\\,[2]=\\{2,9\\},\\,[3]=\\{3\\}\\,[4]=\\{4\\},\\,[5]=\\{5\\},\\,[6]=\\{6\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
