    "q-86235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86235",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\},$ defined by \n\n$$\n\\{(0,0),(1,1),(1,4),(2,2),(3,3),(4,1),(4,4)\\}.\n$$\n\n How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼0\\} \\\\  & =\\{0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼1\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[2] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼2\\} \\\\  & =\\{2\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼3\\} \\\\  & =\\{3\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe  skip $[4]$ since we already know that $4\\in [1]$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $4$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]=\\{0\\},\\,[1]=\\{1,4\\},\\,[2]=\\{2\\},\\,[3]=\\{3\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
