    "q-81662": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81662",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A=\\{0,1,2,…,9\\},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi b}{2}).$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?",
      "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼0\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{2})\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=1\\} \\\\  & =\\{0,4,8\\} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼1\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 1}{2})\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=0\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,3,5,7,9\\} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼2\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 2}{2})\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\} \\\\  & =\\{a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi a}{2})=-1\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,6\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $3$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]=\\{0,4,8\\},\\,[1]=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\},\\,[2]=\\{2,6\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
